{"question_id": "481", "answer": "**\u2016T(\u03b2)\u2016 \u2264 2.**\n\nThis follows because |f(x) \u2212 f(xs)| \u2264 2\u2016f\u2016\u221e for every s, and \u03b2 is a probability measure, so |(T(\u03b2)f)(x)| \u2264 2\u2016f\u2016\u221e.\n\nIf \"exact upper bound\" means the sharpest bound, it can be tightened. The integrand vanishes at s = e, which gives \u2016T(\u03b2)\u2016 \u2264 2(1 \u2212 \u03b2({e})). I am not certain which of the two bounds your source construction states; the standard statement is 2.", "grade": "CORRECT", "call": {"instrument": "grader", "model": "gemini-3.8-flash", "question_id": "481", "tier": 4, "input_tokens": 1677, "output_tokens": 1, "usd": "0.0012615", "latency_s": 3.5532989490020555, "reply": "A", "served_model": "gemini-3.8-flash", "reasoning_tokens": 0, "effort": "", "truncated": false}}
